{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-99113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+3y-z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-99113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=11.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=11\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda x \\\\ 3=2\\lambda y \\\\ -1=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=11$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ \\frac{11}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =11 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=\\pm 3,\\,z=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(1,3,-1),\\,(-1,-3,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,3,-1)$ | $(-1,-3,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $11$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,-3,1),$ and its minimum value is $-11.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-99113" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e3358b94aed395686476ce4fc1104b01baf02d6cfb7a29af125f12ea6a27a41"} {"question" "q-99113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-99113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-11"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-99113" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-11"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-101310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3-y+5z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-101310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=26\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,-1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=26$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =26 \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ \\frac{26}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=∓1\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(0,-1,5),\\,(0,1,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,5)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,-5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,29\\,$ | $\\,-23\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-23.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-101310" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e3358b94aed395686476ce4fc1104b01baf02d6cfb7a29af125f12ea6a27a41"} {"question" "q-101310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-101310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14"] ["math" "-23"] ["math" "-26"] ["math" "-35"] ["math" "-40"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-101310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-23"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=4y-2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4x,4-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 4-2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda +2)=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-5 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\sqrt{5}),(0,\\sqrt{5}).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-2+1)=2\\,⇒\\,y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-5=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-1 & =0\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(1,-2),(-1,-2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm \\sqrt{5}),\\,(\\pm 1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-\\sqrt{5})$ | $(0,\\sqrt{5})$ | $(\\pm 1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $4\\sqrt{5}-5$ | $-14$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 1,-2)$ and its minimum value is $-14.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162763" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e3358b94aed395686476ce4fc1104b01baf02d6cfb7a29af125f12ea6a27a41"} {"question" "q-162763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162763" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-2),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2+1)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(2,0),(-2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-3$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,\\pm 2)$ and its minimum value is $-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e3358b94aed395686476ce4fc1104b01baf02d6cfb7a29af125f12ea6a27a41"} {"question" "q-162764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3$ on the surface ${z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-y,-x,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x=-\\lambda y \\\\ 2y=-\\lambda x \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}(4-{\\lambda }^{2})x=0 \\\\ (4-{\\lambda }^{2})y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 2.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-2.$ Then we must have $x=y$ and $z=0.$ But this also contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =2.$ Then we must have $x=-y$ and $z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-(-y)y-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(∓2,\\pm 2,0).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 2)\\,$ | $\\,(∓2,\\pm 2,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,0,\\pm 2),$ and its minimum value is $7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e3358b94aed395686476ce4fc1104b01baf02d6cfb7a29af125f12ea6a27a41"} {"question" "q-162784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14073453/state.json" "file_sha256" "47e850e0a1b6f925b1cd7b17ba87725335f849c05e3eb5b64f1d8bc2a4661d44"} {"question" "q-162784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value